      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to determine the smallest positive \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, let's recall the definitions of the tangent and cotangent functions:\n\\[\n\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}.\n\\]\nThus, the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) can be rewritten as:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}.\n\\]\n\nTo find the period, we need to determine when the expression \\( \\frac{\\sin(x + T)}{\\cos(x + T)} + \\frac{\\cos(x + T)}{\\sin(x + T)} \\) equals \\( \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\).\n\nLet's consider the periodicity of the trigonometric functions involved. The functions \\( \\sin x \\) and \\( \\cos x \\) have a period of \\( 2\\pi \\). Therefore, we need to check if the periodicity of \\( y \\) is \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nWe start by setting up the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(x + T)}{\\cos(x + T)} + \\frac{\\cos(x + T)}{\\sin(x + T)} = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}.\n\\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\sin(x + T) = \\sin(x + 2\\pi k) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos(x + T) = \\cos(x + 2\\pi k)\n\\]\nfor any integer \\( k \\). This means that \\( x + T \\) and \\( x \\) differ in their input by a multiple of \\( 2\\pi \\), and thus the periodicity condition \\( y(x + 2\\pi) = y(x) \\) must hold.\n\nTo confirm, let's check if \\( y(x + 2\\pi) = y(x) \\):\n\\[\ny(x + 2\\pi) = \\tan(x + 2\\pi) + \\cot(x + 2\\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x = y(x).\n\\]\nThis shows that \\( y(x + 2\\pi) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\), confirming that the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is indeed \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nTherefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
