      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to understand the periodicity of the individual components \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\).\n\n1. **Period of \\(\\tan x\\):**\n   The tangent function, \\(\\tan x\\), is periodic with a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means:\n   \\[\n   \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the period of \\(\\tan x\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. **Period of \\(\\cot x\\):**\n   The cotangent function, \\(\\cot x\\), is also periodic with a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means:\n   \\[\n   \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the period of \\(\\cot x\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\n3. **Period of the sum \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\):**\n   To find the period of the sum \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to find the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   y(x + T) = y(x)\n   \\]\n   for all \\( x \\). Given that both \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) have a period of \\(\\pi\\), we need to check if a smaller period exists.\n\n   Let's check if \\( T = \\pi \\) is indeed the period:\n   \\[\n   y(x + \\pi) = \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi)\n   \\]\n   Using the periodic properties of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   y(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x = y(x)\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( T = \\pi \\) satisfies the periodicity condition.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   Since the smallest positive period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\), we conclude that the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\pi}\n   \\]",
