      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to understand the periodicity of the individual components of the function, namely \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\).\n\n1. **Period of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\):**\n   - The function \\(\\tan x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\) for all \\(x\\).\n   - The function \\(\\cot x\\) also has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\) for all \\(x\\).\n\n2. **Combining the periods:**\n   - Since both \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) have a period of \\(\\pi\\), the function \\(y = \\tan x + \\cot x\\) will repeat its values every \\(\\pi\\). This is because the sum of two functions with periods \\(\\pi\\) will also have a period of \\(\\pi\\).\n\n3. **Verification:**\n   - To verify, consider a \\(3\\pi/2\\) interval, which is half the period of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\), and see if the function repeats:\n     \\[\n     y = \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x = y(x).\n     \\]\n   - Since \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\) for any \\(x\\), \\(y = \\tan x + \\cot x\\) repeats every \\(\\pi\\).\n\nTherefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
